(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-lipid-statins-quiz"]={"title":"Statins & Statin Guidelines Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which apolipoprotein binds LDL (low-density lipoprotein) to its receptor?","choices":["ApoB-100","ApoA-I","ApoC-II","ApoE"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. ApoB-100 is the only apoprotein of LDL and the ligand for the LDL receptor, which clears about 75% of LDL particles, mostly in the liver. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which enzyme do statins inhibit?","choices":["Lipoprotein lipase","Cholesterol 7-alpha hydroxylase","HMG-CoA reductase","PCSK9 in the plasma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase in the hepatic cholesterol synthesis pathway. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which patient with diabetes and no clinical cardiovascular disease meets a statin benefit group?","choices":["Age 30 with LDL of 120 mg/dL","Age 55 with LDL of 60 mg/dL","Age 55 with LDL of 120 mg/dL","Age 82 with LDL of 120 mg/dL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The diabetes group is ages 40 to 75 with LDL (low-density lipoprotein) 70 to 189 mg/dL and no clinical atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which measure reduces the risk of statin myopathy?","choices":["Routinely adding a fibrate","Using the lowest effective dose","Taking it with grapefruit juice","Using the highest tolerated dose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Risk is also reduced by caution in renal impairment, caution with fibrates, avoiding other drug interactions, and monitoring symptoms and laboratory values. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How does the ACC/AHA (American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association) cholesterol guideline differ from ATP III (Adult Treatment Panel III)?","choices":["It targets an LDL below 100 mg/dL","It does not treat to an LDL target","It starts everyone on high intensity","It makes niacin the first choice"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It starts moderate- or high-intensity statin therapy for the four benefit groups and does not titrate to a specific LDL (low-density lipoprotein) target. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the most severe muscle toxicity of statins?","choices":["Myalgia","Muscle cramps at night","Rhabdomyolysis","Essential tremor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Myalgia and myopathy (0.2 to 0.4%) are more common; rhabdomyolysis is rare but the most severe, and muscle toxicity requires stopping the statin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic is listed as a CYP-mediated statin interaction?","choices":["Amiodarone","Digoxin","Esmolol","Sotalol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil, amiodarone, niacin, fibric acid derivatives and grapefruit juice are the listed statin interactions, especially through CYP3A4. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which is a common side effect of statins?","choices":["Cutaneous flushing and itching","Constipation and bloating","Headache and sleep disturbance","Gallstones and abdominal pain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Common statin side effects are headache, sleep disturbance, fatigue, gastrointestinal intolerance, flu-like symptoms and liver enzyme rises. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which of these drugs is an HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor?","choices":["Pitavastatin","Ezetimibe","Gemfibrozil","Colesevelam"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The -statin suffix marks the class: lovastatin, simvastatin, pravastatin, fluvastatin, atorvastatin, rosuvastatin and pitavastatin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In which patient is a statin contraindicated?","choices":["A patient aged 50 with diabetes","A patient after a heart attack","A patient with LDL of 210 mg/dL","A patient who is pregnant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hepatic disease and pregnancy are the statin contraindications. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which statin is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4?","choices":["Rosuvastatin","Pravastatin","Pitavastatin","Simvastatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin are the CYP3A4 statins, so CYP3A4 inhibitors such as verapamil raise their levels. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which lipoprotein carries dietary fat away from the intestine?","choices":["Chylomicrons","Very low-density lipoprotein","Low-density lipoprotein","High-density lipoprotein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chylomicrons are secreted by intestinal cells; lipoprotein lipase hydrolyzes their triglyceride core, and the liver removes the remnants. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which item in a patient's diet interacts with simvastatin through CYP450 metabolism?","choices":["Salt substitutes","Alcohol","Antacids","Grapefruit juice"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Grapefruit juice is listed with verapamil, amiodarone, niacin and fibrates as a statin interaction, especially involving CYP3A4. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 42-year-old with no diabetes and no cardiovascular disease has an LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol of 212 mg/dL. Does he fall into a statin benefit group?","choices":["No, he needs diabetes as well","Yes, LDL above 190 alone qualifies","No, he must be at least 55","No, he needs prior heart disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Individuals with LDL above 190 mg/dL form one of the four statin benefit groups, whatever their other risk factors. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"By how much does high-intensity statin therapy lower LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol?","choices":["By 30 to 49%","By 50% or more","By less than 30%","By 10 to 20%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. High intensity lowers LDL by 50% or more; moderate intensity by 30 to 49%; low intensity by less than 30%. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which statin can be used as high-intensity therapy?","choices":["Pravastatin","Lovastatin","Fluvastatin","Rosuvastatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Only atorvastatin and rosuvastatin reach high intensity, lowering LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol by 50% or more. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 68"}]};
